./continual_arc_rectangle_denoising_notes.md:7:Both demonstrations show an image with a uniform background, many axis-aligned solid rectangular objects in assorted colors (including objects in the demo's color 0), and isolated pixels of other colors that act as corruption. The output removes those stray pixels (replacing them with background) and repairs corruptions inside rectangle objects so the rectangles become solid again.
./continual_arc_rectangle_denoising_notes.md:27:These bounds looked plausible but produced incorrect submissions, so at least one assumption or boundary/value was wrong. In future, use demonstrations to establish the rule, then carefully re-evaluate any apparent rectangle touching/overlapping or interrupted by noise. Don't spend attempts on speculative repeated variants.
./arc_task_notes.md:4:Solved the test after one demo. The grid contains several large monochrome rectangular/irregular regions amid background color 7. Isolated foreign-color pixels are distractor/noise: erase them to the local region's color when inside a region, and to background 7 when outside. Preserve the base shapes and background patches. Demo confirmed this: within the large color-3 and color-0 fields, isolated 9/2/6 markers were changed to the surrounding fill; an isolated 9 in the top-left 3-field was likewise removed. In test, preserve 5 and 9 regions, clean stray marker values (2/4/8/0/3) according to the region/background beneath them, and keep unrelated colored rectangular patches that form coherent regions (e.g. the 9 blocks at lower left and far right).
./memory/continual-arc-line-interpolation.md:11:- The marker color and background color vary by instance; color 3 is the observed fill color in the demos. Identify colors from the current examples rather than assuming fixed marker/background values.
./memory/continual-arc-panel-mask-rule.md:7:In other words, overlay corresponding panels, retaining the left foreground only where the corresponding cell in the right panel is not a hole/zero. Ignore separator and right background color except distinguishing zero holes. Verify panel alignment: separator is one column; panels are equal width. Evidence: demo input 6x25 had 12-column panels separated by col 12; every left 6 at a right-panel zero was erased, other 6s became 2. Solved 16x27 instance had 13-column panels separated by col 13; same intersection rule yielded correct 16x13 output. Avoid simply recoloring the left panel: the sparse zero mask removes pixels at aligned coordinates.
